Consider the time-reversal-invariant Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble (GOE), defined as the ensemble of real symmetric N×N random matrices H=(H_{ij}) whose entries are independent Gaussian random variables (up to symmetry H_{ij}=H_{ji}) with mean zero, variance v^2(1+δ_{ij}), and joint distribution invariant under orthogonal transformations. In the limit N→∞, the GOE predicts a number of universal statistical signatures for the spectrum of a complex quantum system, including:

(a) the Wigner surmise for the distribution of nearest-neighbor level spacings (after unfolding to unit mean spacing), P(s)=(π/2)s exp(−π s^2/4);

(b) the Dyson–Mehta Δ_3(L) statistic, measuring spectral rigidity over a stretch of L consecutive unfolded levels, with the asymptotic GOE form Δ_3(L) ≈ (1/π^2)[ln(2π L)+γ−5/4−π^2/8] for large L;

(c) the Porter–Thomas distribution for partial decay widths Γ, namely a chi-squared distribution with one degree of freedom: P(Γ) dΓ = (2π Γ ⟨Γ⟩)^{−1/2} exp(−Γ/(2⟨Γ⟩)) dΓ, obtained from the assumption that the components of an eigenvector along any fixed channel vector are independent Gaussian random variables.

Now consider the experimental compound-nucleus resonance data: large sets of neutron and proton resonances of fixed spin and parity (so-called quantum-number-clean sequences) measured for medium and heavy nuclei, together with their resonance positions (giving spacings) and reduced partial widths (in particular the reduced neutron widths Γ_n^0).

Question: Are the GOE predictions (a), (b), and (c) consistent with the experimental data on compound-nucleus resonances—across nearest-neighbor spacing distributions, the long-range Δ_3(L) rigidity for long resonance sequences, and the distribution of reduced neutron widths—at a level of agreement strong enough to justify random-matrix theory as a physically meaningful description of the compound-nucleus regime in nuclear reaction theory?

